{:edb/db-before-t 2787 :edb/db-after-t 2788 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536100 50 #inst "2026-10-09T14:59:40.628Z" 13194139536100 true] [17592186161735 1100 "Find the principal axes of the curve $19x_1^2-6x_1x_2+11x_2^2 = 20$." 13194139536100 false] [17592186161735 1100 "Find the principal axes of the curve $19x_1^2-6x_1x_2+11x_2^2 = 20.$" 13194139536100 true] [17592186161735 1145 "First, consider the quadratic form\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=19x_1^2-6x_1x_2+11x_2^2\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 20\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A$.\n\nBy solving the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\mid A-\\lambda I \\mid = 0\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=20 \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad \\lambda_2=10\n$$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=20$, we have\n$A - 20I|= \\begin{bmatrix}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{bmatrix} - 20\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-20I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2 \\| = \\sqrt{10}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=10$, we have\n$A - 10I|= \\begin{bmatrix}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{bmatrix} - 10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{10}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139536100 false] [17592186161735 1145 "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=19x_1^2-6x_1x_2+11x_2^2.$ The matrix of the form is \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/31f8c54fecdb8b2e8b3f3cad20a642a39eab9ae2987d49849a1c1dabc34e5368.png)\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 20\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I| = 0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=20 \\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_2=10.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=20,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-20I & =[\\begin{aligned}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{aligned}]-20[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-20I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/51897cac20d424f599eba1c8aada5259279ab9ad263e7557d6614ce79e68fa0f.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{10},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/f9913f9139af447be912bccc2350f086ce32bae89849495f8e82a3eebf8a7918.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/2cdd8527be173b17e254bd4ecda571d71b15938c3a1f6cb2609122db2b10c64d.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{10},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/f6764ef3200afce932740e8100915018b6a0c21e902d932b218e79bcd57e0b95.png)\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\qquad$ ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/1549a73afb90f3fa0fb8218d9d63daa1a5534c3e546613cbf3f226371b8f1092.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/d4a6e3f0c138e6dd83252df8adc14359ba67ae77dec0270264ea6d00c7b08667.png)" 13194139536100 true] [17592186161736 1100 "Which curve is defined by the equation $6x_1^2+8x_1x_2-9x_2^2= 70$?" 13194139536100 false] [17592186161736 1100 "Which curve is defined by the equation $6x_1^2+8x_1x_2-9x_2^2= 70?$" 13194139536100 true] [17592186161736 1145 "Consider the quadratic form\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=6x_1^2+8x_1x_2-9x_2^2\n$$\n\nTo determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A - λI \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}6 - λ & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 3λ - 70 &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= 7,-10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $7y_1^2 -10 y_2^2$.\n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7y_{1}^{2} - 10y_{2}^{2} &= 70 \\\\\n\\frac{y_{1}^{2}}{10} - \\frac{y_{2}^{2}}{7} &= 1 \\\\\n(y_{1}^{2})/((\\sqrt{10})^{2}) - (y_{2}^{2})/((\\sqrt{7})^{2}) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{10}$ and $\\sqrt{7}$." 13194139536100 false] [17592186161736 1145 "Consider the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=6x_1^2+8x_1x_2-9x_2^2.$ To  determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ which is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/33a30f2c07875359a60caed74f802a2de29ac9e44089800ed8f30a2015303bce.png)\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -70 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =7,\\,-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $7y_1^2 -10 y_2^2.$  \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{y}_{1}^{2}-10{y}_{2}^{2} & =70 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{7} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{10}$ and $\\sqrt{7}.$" 13194139536100 true] [17592186161750 1100 "Draw a diagram that depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/5354b2fa6d2fb2c8b1b9ea3cb675225619e8debe64e1ec108b556248d6e672ec.png)" 13194139536100 true] [17592186161750 1100 "Draw a diagram that depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3129/Source/Images/fa1f2ef5edfdc2471cf95a125c9702a1.png)" 13194139536100 false] [17592186161750 1145 "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation.\n\nOur curve is a hyperbola. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nif one axis passes through the vertices of the hyperbola and the other axis is the perpendicular bisector of the segment connecting the two vertices.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3129/Source/Images/2503cf232548072ca23d6152156ff107.png)" 13194139536100 false] [17592186161750 1145 "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation. \n\nOur curve is a hyperbola. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nif one axis passes through the vertices of the hyperbola and the other axis is the perpendicular bisector of the segment connecting the two vertices.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/bd0d117543012be1c4eab0acda878c9aea67518e46c93048e8c249de1a0c03e6.png)" 13194139536100 true] [17592186161773 1100 "Consider the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/4114e6536f57106949ed39a6ae4979646a0dd58c5690d7e7bcf9ffed15de8086.png) and the quadratic curve $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 1.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=1,$ find the principal axes of the curve." 13194139536100 true] [17592186161773 1100 "Consider the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and the quadratic curve $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 1$. Given that $A$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=1$, find the principal axes of the curve." 13194139536100 false] [17592186161773 1145 "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 1\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A$. Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3$, we have\n$A - 3I|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} - 3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking 