\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/e0d71ad9216b9fa7c1831e6874b9a97bba9c1d9c5dbd6d9664635ed468d82a4c.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/83fd398b476c1c749346754a05c7f4071a98ab61453405a99ad8fcff65e38aaf.png)\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\qquad$ ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/8f0d84bb5c3ae37714c781b5dafbba1ae7de8561234ca558d3da2dcbfa9d75cf.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/293ad98697d595e3e0049aae93e54209b6d17aa92eb131b57791f211167690fc.png)" 13194139536100 true] [17592186161943 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383558 13194139536100 true] [17592186161943 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383565 13194139536100 true] [17592186161943 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383572 13194139536100 true] [17592186161943 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383579 13194139536100 true] [17592186161943 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383586 13194139536100 true] [17592186161944 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383530 13194139536100 true] [17592186161944 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383537 13194139536100 true] [17592186161944 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383544 13194139536100 true] [17592186161944 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383551 13194139536100 true] [17592186161958 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383593 13194139536100 true] [17592186161958 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383600 13194139536100 true] [17592186161958 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383607 13194139536100 true] [17592186161958 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383614 13194139536100 true] [17592186161979 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383621 13194139536100 true] [17592186161979 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383628 13194139536100 true] [17592186161979 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383635 13194139536100 true] [17592186161979 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383642 13194139536100 true] [17592186161979 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383649 13194139536100 true] [17592186162568 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536100 true] [17592186162568 1100 "Which curve is defined by the equation $2x_{1}^{2} - 6x_{1}x_{2} + 10x_{2}^{2} = 44$?" 13194139536100 false] [17592186162568 1100 "Which curve is defined by the equation $2{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+10{x}_{2}^{2}=44?$" 13194139536100 true] [17592186162568 1145 "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+10{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 10-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +11 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =11,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $11{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} & =44 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{44} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{11})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $2\\sqrt{11}.$" 13194139536100 true] [17592186162607 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536100 true] [17592186162607 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/7c810e01753fea6e8b401acec4c69cab624f3ac9686db7e88dc1093b3675812f.png)\n\nWhich of the following diagrams depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown above?" 13194139536100 true] [17592186162607 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3129/Source/Images/q-96031.png)\n\nWhich of the following diagrams depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown above?" 13194139536100 false] [17592186162607 1145 "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation. \n\nOur curve is a hyperbola. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nif one axis passes through the vertices of the hyperbola and the other axis is the perpendicular bisector of the segment connecting the two vertices.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/7ccbc3dcc39f79dab841539230fc82112384027013a719ea02350174ec566d78.png)" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383530 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383531 13194139536100 true] [17592186383530 1089 "q-140549" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383530 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536100 true] [17592186383530 1100 "Which curve is defined by the equation $3{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}=24?$" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383530 1113 #uuid "042ead02-bb3a-5e4c-9374-7ae765cba5f9" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383530 1145 "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -24 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $4{y}_{1}^{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}_{1}^{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{6}$ and $2.$" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383531 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383532 13194139536100 true] [17592186383531 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383533 13194139536100 true] [17592186383531 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383534 13194139536100 true] [17592186383531 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383535 13194139536100 true] [17592186383531 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383536 13194139536100 true] [17592186383531 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536100 true] [17592186383531 1041 "selection" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383531 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383533 13194139536100 true] [17592186383531 1110 #uuid "8df24f14-2ef2-533c-908d-480d4f782c93" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383532 1034 "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{6}$ and $2$" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383532 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536100 true] [17592186383532 1111 #uuid "50a727c0-0c69-5cde-bd49-a23ec0c6563f" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383533 1034 "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{6}$ and $2$" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383533 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536100 true] [17592186383533 1111 #uuid "6c994f35-b46d-5c1c-93e7-2ac8178cae09" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383534 1034 "Two parallel lines" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383534 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536100 true] [17592186383534 1111 #uuid "aca36349-4f7f-536a-a6bd-a34442139674" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383535 1034 "An ellipse with semiaxes $6$ and $4$" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383535 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536100 true] [17592186383535 1111 #uuid "757b6751-ea84-5cfc-8e9e-651cd3229119" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383536 1034 "A hyperbola with semiaxes $6$ and $4$" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383536 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536100 true] [17592186