[17592186383618 1111 #uuid "3f5cafb7-2bc1-5e3a-966f-27b43791a34c" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383619 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/6aeb70c5041be20ab9c38724e3cf1e1852f491478c32037222beb83372ab7776.png" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383619 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139536100 true] [17592186383619 1111 #uuid "9193abb2-b6dd-59e3-9de9-b4517688f284" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383620 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/ae362c4e6d675c8d7ca65f2aa3702d65c63b0ffb6e370a573da00532e567ab1c.png" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383620 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139536100 true] [17592186383620 1111 #uuid "868c5cee-1d34-51d1-8317-c6e08550f250" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383621 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383622 13194139536100 true] [17592186383621 1089 "q-97988" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383621 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536100 true] [17592186383621 1100 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=2.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ find the principal axes of the curve." 13194139536100 true] [17592186383621 1113 #uuid "334c09b2-8068-5ab6-b14b-135919cc8d1e" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383621 1145 "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=2\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383623 13194139536100 true] [17592186383622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383624 13194139536100 true] [17592186383622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383625 13194139536100 true] [17592186383622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383626 13194139536100 true] [17592186383622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383627 13194139536100 true] [17592186383622 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536100 true] [17592186383622 1041 "selection" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383622 1104 #edb/ref 17592186383625 13194139536100 true] [17592186383622 1110 #uuid "bc22569a-e1a1-5369-b389-2256bf925a0d" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383623 1034 "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383623 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536100 true] [17592186383623 1111 #uuid "867c17e6-cfae-582d-b5f0-90f978d62307" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383624 1034 "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383624 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536100 true] [17592186383624 1111 #uuid "ffd0be3d-db64-5c4c-8eb1-7d96a2508060" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383625 1034 "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383625 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536100 true] [17592186383625 1111 #uuid "1f56209b-e044-532c-aeda-543574ca20de" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383626 1034 "$\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383626 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536100 true] [17592186383626 1111 #uuid "83d3d754-4357-50e6-b7fc-6b21cebc59d0" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383627 1034 "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383627 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536100 true] [17592186383627 1111 #uuid "6d1e733a-2c19-5f3d-9098-9092978a7d91" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383628 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383629 13194139536100 true] [17592186383628 1089 "q-97990" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383628 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536100 true] [17592186383628 1100 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=7.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ find the principal axes of the curve." 13194139536100 true] [17592186383628 1113 #uuid "5fb95d9e-0ced-5515-baa2-9595c0717556" 13194139536100 true] [17592186383628 1145 "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=7\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\en